# Ownership for the Tier 3 workloads repo.
#
# Replace @stadionmoney with your actual GitHub organization. A bare @sysops
# would be read as a USERNAME — team references require the org prefix.
#
# A team must also have at least Write access to this repo
# (Settings -> Collaborators and teams). A team without repo access is silently
# ignored here, with no warning anywhere.
#
# This file is advisory unless branch protection on `main` has both
# "Require a pull request before merging" and
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled.
#
# Last matching pattern wins — put narrower paths later when splitting
# ownership.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS REPO EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM THE INFRA REPO
#
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md in the infra repo splits Tier 2/3 out so that app teams
# can own their own workloads without being able to change networking, keys or
# permission boundaries. That split only means anything if the ownership lines
# below are real - two repos with the same reviewers is just two repos.
#
# So the intended end state is:
#
#   ci/, pipelines/, docs/   SysOps only. These decide HOW anything deploys.
#   tier3-workloads/<app>/   the team that owns that app, plus SysOps
#
# Until app teams exist, SysOps owns all of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# * @stadionmoney/sysops

# The deployment machinery stays with SysOps even once app teams own their own
# directories above. An app team that can edit ci/ can deploy anything, which
# would undo the whole reason for the repo split.
# /ci/        @stadionmoney/sysops
# /pipelines/ @stadionmoney/sysops
# /docs/      @stadionmoney/sysops
